WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.187
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.187 --> 00:00:12.965
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:12.965 --> 00:00:15.496
&gt;&gt; Let's see, good afternoon.

00:00:15.496 --> 00:00:16.611
&gt;&gt; Good afternoon.

00:00:16.611 --> 00:00:20.163
&gt;&gt; At Walmart,
we tend to speak back to each other.

00:00:20.163 --> 00:00:22.430
We're going to have some time
in a little bit to talk so

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:25.130
please be thinking about what
you might want to ask us about.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.210
I'm really excited,
Indra, that you're here.

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:27.770
&gt;&gt; I am too.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.505
&gt;&gt; They told me I couldn't come
back unless I brought you with me.

00:00:30.505 --> 00:00:31.080
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.318
I said I'd come back only if you
were going to do it with me.

00:00:33.318 --> 00:00:36.286
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I want to start with maybe the most

00:00:36.286 --> 00:00:39.902
important question, and that is,
when you're in your University and

00:00:39.902 --> 00:00:43.901
you were playing cricket, what position
did you plan on the university team?

00:00:43.901 --> 00:00:46.611
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You know, first of all,

00:00:46.611 --> 00:00:48.197
do you know what cricket is all about?

00:00:48.197 --> 00:00:48.893
&gt;&gt; I do not.

00:00:48.893 --> 00:00:50.193
And I-
&gt;&gt; Okay, so.

00:00:50.193 --> 00:00:51.216
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Still not

00:00:51.216 --> 00:00:52.701
going to make a difference
what I tell you.

00:00:52.701 --> 00:00:54.745
&gt;&gt; I'm not really very interested either.

00:00:54.745 --> 00:00:55.418
&gt;&gt; Okay, okay.

00:00:55.418 --> 00:00:58.460
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Go ahead.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:00.540
Let me-
&gt;&gt; What position did you placed here?

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.751
&gt;&gt; I opened the busing and the bowling.

00:01:01.751 --> 00:01:04.514
[SOUND]
&gt;&gt; Someone knows what that means.

00:01:04.514 --> 00:01:05.648
It's really great.

00:01:05.648 --> 00:01:06.453
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:06.453 --> 00:01:06.963
&gt;&gt; Are you impress?

00:01:06.963 --> 00:01:07.846
&gt;&gt; Leadership.
&gt;&gt; Are you impress?

00:01:07.846 --> 00:01:09.030
&gt;&gt; I think so, yeah.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:13.380
&gt;&gt; Then, I was also the captain
of the Cricket team.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:14.540
&gt;&gt; That was kind of my point.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:15.495
She's a leader.

00:01:15.495 --> 00:01:18.870
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I have known Indra for a really

00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.070
long time, and I was thinking about
what words I would use to describe her.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:28.371
And some of them would be, visionary,
competitive, tough-minded, caring.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.260
&gt;&gt; Indra makes things happen.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.151
She has a natural leadership
characteristic that you have

00:01:34.151 --> 00:01:37.160
used not only to lead PepsiCo and
in your previous roles, but

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.230
you also influenced Walmart in a big way.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.205
&gt;&gt; Mm.

00:01:40.205 --> 00:01:43.690
I appreciate it, Doug, but
let me give you the compliment back.

00:01:43.690 --> 00:01:46.710
Doug and I know each other for
a long, long time.

00:01:46.710 --> 00:01:50.150
And I've always referred to
Doug as not just Doug McMillon,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:54.520
I'd call him Doug McMillon Walton,
because he represents the best of Walmart,

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.700
the values of Walmart.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:59.476
And believe me, even though we're
a generation apart in age, Doug.

00:01:59.476 --> 00:02:01.990
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm not sure.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.230
&gt;&gt; I'm much older.

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:07.716
I've learned so much from Doug,
the way he operates this large company.

00:02:07.716 --> 00:02:08.980
And is repositioning it, and so

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:13.495
I think this is a two person back slapping
society and I'm the charter member of it.

00:02:13.495 --> 00:02:14.166
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:02:14.166 --> 00:02:19.004
as we think about what these people might
be interested in or benefit from the most,

00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:21.780
the first thing that comes
to my mind is change.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:25.920
And I know your business well enough and
know what you're thinking well enough to

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:31.200
know that you're facing a situation where
the world is changing very dramatically.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:35.180
People are changing, the environment
you're operating is changing and

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:38.550
you're trying to position PepsiCo for
the future.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.100
So maybe we start out with
starting out talking about

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:45.030
what you see that convinces you
that PepsiCo needs a change and

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.680
what is it that you are trying to
accomplish during your tenure.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:53.440
&gt;&gt; It's many questions nested in one but
let me take a shot at it.

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.285
I think every company today,
the CEOs of every company,

00:02:57.285 --> 00:03:01.660
are looking at the environment and
sort of the investor expects you

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:06.130
to be this duck that's just sort
of swimming across the water.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.750
And in the past these ducks
would sort of furiously

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:12.180
swim underneath the water
just to move ahead.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.340
Now, it's like you've got to paddle on
steroids because the pace of change is so

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:22.060
enormous that you've got to paddle even
faster just to make small movements.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.290
And nobody is going to give you
a break and say you can slow down or

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:27.570
you can actually stay in place for awhile.

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:28.550
What's changing?

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:29.580
And I'll talk about the food and

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:33.490
beverage industry because in a way
our interests intersect there.

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:35.560
Every aspect of the business is changing.

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:39.970
What is healthy food is changing.

00:03:39.970 --> 00:03:43.350
What do people want to eat and
drink is changing.

00:03:43.350 --> 00:03:47.060
How should products be
labeled has not been defined.

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:48.639
There is no authority that defines it.

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:53.770
Supply change are globalizing yet
traceability and

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:56.720
safety standards have
not been established.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:03:58.510
There's an NGO in every corner.

00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:01.470
So, there's somebody there calling us
out when don't do things right but

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:05.190
then there's no standard to look at
in terms of what we need to do right.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.870
The retail environment is changing
whether it's the emergence of

00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:14.860
Walmart in the 90s and the year 2000s or
now the hard discounters and

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:16.840
the emergence of e-commerce.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:18.950
Everything about the world is changing.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:23.390
And then, right here in Silicon Valley,
all of the best and

00:04:23.390 --> 00:04:27.050
brightest that we want in our companies
are being recruited by these startups.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.160
So we have a tough time recruiting
the right talent to run our companies.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.960
I'm speaking to all of you, so
I think, what happens is that,

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:39.120
we are facing unprecedented
change in our business models.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.485
And on top of that, we have to globalize,
but the world is a tough place right now.

00:04:43.485 --> 00:04:48.030
We've got economic volatility,
macro-economic crises in many,

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.210
many places, political upheaval.

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:57.180
So I think companies like ours,
if you really want to keep performing,

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:00.228
you've almost gotta change
your model all the time.

00:05:00.228 --> 00:05:03.410
And Klaus Schwab of the world
economic forum talks about this and

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:05.510
says, you've always gotta be in beta.

00:05:05.510 --> 00:05:08.420
It sounds wonderful as a set of words, but

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.980
when you actually have to practice
in the company, it's pretty scary.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:15.450
Because it's not motivating
a small group of start ups or

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.478
a small group of employees who have
the same thinking that you do.

00:05:18.478 --> 00:05:23.210
We have 260,000 employees and
when you decide to zag and

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:26.940
zig you got to tell them what you're
going to be doing, why you're doing it.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:28.620
How it's going to impact them and

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:31.280
why they all have to some
along with you on the journey.

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:36.820
Let me give you examples when we decided
to change our product portfolio to go for

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:41.670
a predominantly fun for you product
line to including more better for

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.460
you, good for you products.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:47.960
Different profitability, very different
product line, closer to agriculture.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:51.840
Changed the model in the company.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.460
Our employees had to go,
what is she doing?

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:55.650
Why do we have to change?

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:59.690
We're doing pretty well selling these
products, why do we have to change?

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:03.170
But the challenge of the leader
is looking around the corner and

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:06.300
making the change before it's
too late to make the change.

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.000
I remember as late as 2007 or 2008.

00:06:10.000 --> 00:06:11.370
Going to see investors.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:14.520
And they looked at me and said,
why do you have to change your portfolio?

00:06:16.110 --> 00:06:16.950
We are American.

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:18.350
We like soda and chips.

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.360
Your job is to sell more soda and chips.

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.720
And I sat there in shock,
because every trend that I was seeing,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:28.200
indicated that the consumption of
these categories was slowing down.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.240
And I looked at the investor and
I said have you changed your eating and

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:32.010
drinking habits.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:36.410
And that person said yeah, but
forget me I'm not the normal consumer.

00:06:36.410 --> 00:06:39.140
I said the problem is many
consumers are like you.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:41.530
They've changed their eating and
drinking habits.

00:06:41.530 --> 00:06:44.610
Today, that same investor says
to me why can't you go faster.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.170
Why you so
slow at making a portfolio change?

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:50.730
It's because people like you wrote
a ballast for me, you didn't let me go

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:55.960
faster because you always criticize what I
was doing, all right, but that's reality.

00:06:55.960 --> 00:06:59.430
So I think we're in a world
of unprecedented change

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:05.310
where companies, the CEOs,
the DNA of the company, has to change.

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:09.650
And we all have to change at a point
when short term is abundance.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.810
Everybody wants the results today.

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:19.270
And the tolerance you have to make
the change on a large scale is not there.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.100
And I'm sure you're facing
the same issues, Doug.

00:07:21.100 --> 00:07:23.650
And I mean,
I watch it in action at Walmart.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:26.380
So tell me a bit about
what you're going through?

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.778
I wanted to ask you the questions.

00:07:27.778 --> 00:07:30.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I already have another one.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:31.020
&gt;&gt; Either way.

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:32.380
&gt;&gt; I think it’s very similar.

00:07:32.380 --> 00:07:35.330
I mean you know where you are and
you develop a vision for

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:39.015
where you need to be and trying to get
from here to there is the hard part.

00:07:39.015 --> 00:07:42.615
I feel like that the vision that we have,
collectively, is improving all the time.

00:07:42.615 --> 00:07:46.715
But turning around and moving an
organization that's older than 50 years,

00:07:46.715 --> 00:07:47.646
that was built for

00:07:47.646 --> 00:07:52.314
certain purpose, with 2.3 million people,
is a big part of the challenge.

00:07:52.314 --> 00:07:55.728
And the last time I was here,
I admitted that I have envy for

00:07:55.728 --> 00:08:00.047
those who were at a startup, because
you can write your culture on the wall,

00:08:00.047 --> 00:08:03.529
or your values on the board,
and you can go that direction,

00:08:03.529 --> 00:08:07.173
you get to create something
rather than change something.

00:08:07.173 --> 00:08:09.074
And changing something feels harder.

00:08:09.074 --> 00:08:11.881
And then I said to anyone who wants
a great challenge, let me know and

00:08:11.881 --> 00:08:13.070
we'll hire you at Walmart.

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:14.682
I had one person call me.

00:08:14.682 --> 00:08:18.160
[LAUGH] So, you know, it is hard and

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:21.016
I think these people are smart
enough to figure that out.

00:08:21.016 --> 00:08:22.144
&gt;&gt; That's true.

00:08:22.144 --> 00:08:23.300
&gt;&gt; Let's go to learning.

00:08:24.990 --> 00:08:32.080
You mentioned that you're seeing what
you believe the company should become.

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.532
You didn't arrive in this position
knowing everything that you know now.

00:08:35.532 --> 00:08:40.257
How do you go about learning as
an individual and using the knowledge that

00:08:40.257 --> 00:08:45.214
you are picking up to shape the future
because as you know strategic planning

00:08:45.214 --> 00:08:49.960
cycles are gone now, its strategy is
being shaped hourly not annually.

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:55.780
&gt;&gt; The one big lesson I've learned,
Doug, is that if you just depend on

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:00.710
the traditional strategic planning cycle,
or depend on a standard set of consulting

00:09:00.710 --> 00:09:04.590
reports, you actually are going to
do your company a big disservice.

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:08.130
And our CEOs and
leaders have to be life long students and

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:13.500
not just students in sense of attending
courses or reading a book or two.

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:17.570
You've gotta learn how to read widely,
walk the market,

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:22.490
look at trends in the market place,
make connections that don't seem obvious.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:26.260
And start to paint pictures
of what the future could be.

00:09:26.260 --> 00:09:28.940
And then watch consumer behavior.

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:29.884
I'll give you an example.

00:09:29.884 --> 00:09:33.670
Ten years ago or maybe 15 years
ago if you came to PepsiCo and

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:36.520
looked at all the beverages that
we're being served on the side

00:09:36.520 --> 00:09:38.600
tables when we're having meetings.

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:43.710
Most of the people will be
consuming full sugar beverages.

00:09:43.710 --> 00:09:46.030
About ten years ago the trend shifted.

00:09:46.030 --> 00:09:49.045
Maybe 20 years ago they
were drinking full sugar.

00:09:49.045 --> 00:09:51.000
Ten years ago they shifted to diet drinks.

00:09:53.630 --> 00:09:55.910
But five years ago they were
drinking more bottled water.

00:09:56.950 --> 00:10:00.220
Now the point I make to
my guys is guys don't

00:10:00.220 --> 00:10:02.570
you don't need to hire a consultant
to tell you where the trends are.

00:10:02.570 --> 00:10:04.920
You just have to look at our side table.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.200
Remember the number of
faces we use to have for

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:11.500
regular sugar Pepsi versus diet
Pepsi versus bottled water now.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:15.420
If you're all drinking bottled water or
drinking zero calorie beverages,

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:18.390
why do we think we're not
representative of the consumer?

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:21.730
Very often I think what
happens is we separate out

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:23.936
the consumer out there
from the consumer in us.

00:10:23.936 --> 00:10:25.550
We are the consumer.

00:10:25.550 --> 00:10:29.500
And the more we can bring the two together
and say we too are representative of

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:33.700
the consumer outside,
I think it will be better we go out and

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:38.150
our children play with other children,
we see other families.

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:42.000
And when they moderate certain
products that we sell,

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:45.330
in terms of what's given to kids,
that's a trend, okay?

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.180
So, we've gotta watch all of these things.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:51.890
And too often, I think, us leaders
think what consultants tell us, or

00:10:51.890 --> 00:10:56.030
what certain reports say, or
certain books say, is the gospel.

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:57.420
It's really not.

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:04.950
We have to become our own data collectors,
data analyzers and then shape creators.

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:10.425
And I think that's the toughest thing
because people who are rewriting the rules

00:11:10.425 --> 00:11:16.141
have to create the shapes and people don't
like that because they have a traditional

00:11:16.141 --> 00:11:21.211
model of how things work and they hate
people who upset status quo and I think

00:11:21.211 --> 00:11:26.406
both of us are upsetting status quo in
a big way and it's tough but it's fun.

00:11:26.406 --> 00:11:27.451
&gt;&gt; It is.

00:11:27.451 --> 00:11:32.179
It's sometime to take your kind a business
hat off and be a customer or consumer and

00:11:32.179 --> 00:11:36.359
I'll admit when I'm working,
like yesterday I went to Palm Springs and

00:11:36.359 --> 00:11:39.648
visited a Super Center,
I'll put my Walmart badge on and

00:11:39.648 --> 00:11:42.760
I walk into that store, and
I have a certain mindset.

00:11:44.190 --> 00:11:45.920
On the weekend, I went shopping.

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:48.450
Because we needed milk and
some things at home.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:51.215
And my vision of that store-
&gt;&gt; Did you go to Walmart?

00:11:51.215 --> 00:11:52.294
&gt;&gt; Of course.

00:11:52.294 --> 00:11:59.805
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
I'm not stupid, I mean.

00:11:59.805 --> 00:12:02.170
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
But

00:12:02.170 --> 00:12:06.930
I just experienced out of stocks
differently, everything felt different.

00:12:06.930 --> 00:12:10.090
And staying objective and
being in the moment is something

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:12.880
that I continue to work on whether
it's a mobile app or the store.

00:12:12.880 --> 00:12:14.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I do market tours every weekend.

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:17.200
The first few years that I was a CEO,

00:12:17.200 --> 00:12:22.240
I'd take pictures of what I saw on the
store and I'd download it to my people and

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:25.140
say, the data stops here,
this doesn't look right.

00:12:25.140 --> 00:12:29.500
I mean I'd go on market
tours sort of about a, two,

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.260
three hour radius from my home.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:37.340
Then I realized that every
division in the US had some people

00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:41.990
waiting in the office for my emails to
respond to that quickly and I thought it

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:45.710
was a terrible way to run a company, that
a CEO's out in the market and you've got

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:50.490
a SWAT team sitting in the office waiting
for the lousy photographs to come in.

00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:53.740
So I stopped sending the photographs,
but I still do the market too, and

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:54.510
I agonize over it.

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:55.740
Just like you,

00:12:55.740 --> 00:13:00.170
I go why is the Gatorade out of stock
when the temperature's 75 degrees.

00:13:00.170 --> 00:13:02.730
Why didn't we have a second delivery,
or why didn't we do

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:07.340
a second delivery of Frito Lay products
when we have a holiday weekend?

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:09.800
And now I'm just learning to
just agonize internally and

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:11.301
dump on people Monday-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:11.301 --> 00:13:13.957
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] It's tough, I gotta tell you,

00:13:13.957 --> 00:13:15.020
it's tough.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:21.800
But when you do that you really experience
your business from the consumer's eyes,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:26.060
all the retailer's eyes, because I
know how you care about other stocks.

00:13:26.060 --> 00:13:29.770
So, when I go to the Norwalk,
Walmart or look at the shelves and

00:13:29.770 --> 00:13:31.400
I see out of stocks.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:32.035
I feel for you.

00:13:32.035 --> 00:13:34.730
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; As I do for me.

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:35.930
&gt;&gt; Our in stock set by the way.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:36.805
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:13:36.805 --> 00:13:39.730
&gt;&gt; Let's talk about design for a minute.

00:13:39.730 --> 00:13:41.830
A month or
two ago we were talking on the phone,

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:45.220
and you tried to get met to go to Italy,
or was it Milan?

00:13:45.220 --> 00:13:46.930
&gt;&gt; The Milan Design Show.

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:50.140
&gt;&gt; Which I thought was kind
of a surprising invitation,

00:13:50.140 --> 00:13:52.625
I don't get invited to
Milan very frequently.

00:13:52.625 --> 00:13:54.850
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You mean you missed a great one.

00:13:54.850 --> 00:13:58.280
&gt;&gt; Tell me, why did you go and why were
you thinking that I should also be there?

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:01.170
&gt;&gt; Do you know anything
about the Milan design show?

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:02.855
&gt;&gt; Only what you told me on the phone.

00:14:02.855 --> 00:14:05.320
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Okay, let me tell you.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:08.542
A few years ago, we started
a design capability inside PepsiCo.

00:14:08.542 --> 00:14:10.840
What do I mean by design capability?

00:14:10.840 --> 00:14:16.130
And I have to tell you a little story
to bring you into the design loop.

00:14:16.130 --> 00:14:19.375
When I first became CEO in 2006,
as I looked at our products,

00:14:19.375 --> 00:14:23.600
I looked at our packaging, I looked
at how we put things on the shelf.

00:14:23.600 --> 00:14:29.370
I thought they were singularly all
right and in some cases unimpressive.

00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:33.930
And I thought that we need a dash
of design thinking in the company.

00:14:33.930 --> 00:14:36.885
So I gave all of my direct reports,
about 16 of them,

00:14:36.885 --> 00:14:40.052
a photo album, a blank photo album and
a camera and I said.

00:14:40.052 --> 00:14:43.903
For the next three months, I want you to
go around taking a picture of anything

00:14:43.903 --> 00:14:46.160
that you think constitutes design.

00:14:46.160 --> 00:14:50.660
Anything, it could be a pencil,
it could be a clip, it could be a teapot.

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:51.970
Anything, anything you want.

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.920
Just fill this book for
the next three months and give me a copy.

00:14:55.920 --> 00:14:56.600
Give it to me so

00:14:56.600 --> 00:15:02.250
that I know what the issues are I have
to handle in terms of design thinking.

00:15:02.250 --> 00:15:04.580
Here's the surprising finding.

00:15:04.580 --> 00:15:07.090
Half the people didn't
turn the book in at all.

00:15:08.270 --> 00:15:09.760
Half the executives.

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:14.246
The few that turned it in, a few of them
had their wives fill it in for them.

00:15:14.246 --> 00:15:17.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Two executives hired a professional

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:19.110
designer to fill the book in.

00:15:19.110 --> 00:15:20.960
And told me they did.

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:25.137
But the few that actually filled in
the book I still have them with me as

00:15:25.137 --> 00:15:26.949
a mementos of how bad we were.

00:15:26.949 --> 00:15:33.380
It was a horror because what they thought
was design was not what design was.

00:15:33.380 --> 00:15:35.579
So let me now tell you what is design?

00:15:35.579 --> 00:15:40.612
To me design is something that you
embed into a product or a service or

00:15:40.612 --> 00:15:45.646
an offering that romances the consumer and
draws them to the shelf or

00:15:45.646 --> 00:15:50.090
to the product, or
to the service or whatever it is.

00:15:50.090 --> 00:15:54.926
It's how you think about the shape
of the product, how it's presented,

00:15:54.926 --> 00:15:56.567
how it's talked about.

00:15:56.567 --> 00:16:00.898
Not just the color of the package
everything in terms of the experience that

00:16:00.898 --> 00:16:02.835
actually draws the consumer in.

00:16:02.835 --> 00:16:04.838
And we never though about design but

00:16:04.838 --> 00:16:08.324
really thought about it as Is
the yellow on the Lays bag right?

00:16:08.324 --> 00:16:10.389
Should we put an extra swirl of red?

00:16:10.389 --> 00:16:13.900
Or should we put an extra
emoji on the Pepsi bottle?

00:16:13.900 --> 00:16:17.560
So, our definition of design was very,
very, I hate to use the word,

00:16:17.560 --> 00:16:18.600
but primitive.

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.260
And I was part of that group.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:23.370
So we all went to design school and
learned all about design.

00:16:23.370 --> 00:16:28.620
Not in terms of the package, but
all the way down into design of a product.

00:16:28.620 --> 00:16:33.380
Example, when you eat Doritos,
how many of you are Doritos consumer here?

00:16:33.380 --> 00:16:35.280
I love it.

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:40.480
So when you eat Doritos,
when you get to the bottom of the bag,

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:43.770
a typical guy would take
the Doritos bag and just do this.

00:16:43.770 --> 00:16:44.640
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Okay?

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:46.810
Because you sort of eat
the crumbs that way.

00:16:46.810 --> 00:16:48.290
Women don't do that.

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:51.753
Women try to be very delicate putting
their hand and taking the last Doritos.

00:16:51.753 --> 00:16:55.863
But then you're leaving about 5%
of the Doritos in the bag, okay?

00:16:55.863 --> 00:16:56.747
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:16:56.747 --> 00:16:59.760
because they're little crumbs there,
which taste awesome.

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:04.750
But in our package design, why don't we
figure out a product design for Doritos?

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:08.666
That is completely different that a woman
can actually put in a put in a purse, so

00:17:08.666 --> 00:17:12.524
that it doesn't crumple, and you can
actually eat it in a more delicate way but

00:17:12.524 --> 00:17:14.008
all the way down to last 5%.

00:17:14.008 --> 00:17:15.086
I'm using Doritos.

00:17:15.086 --> 00:17:17.199
&gt;&gt; Indra is solving real
world problems here.

00:17:17.199 --> 00:17:17.761
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:17:17.761 --> 00:17:18.528
&gt;&gt; Right in front of you.

00:17:18.528 --> 00:17:19.085
&gt;&gt; Doug.

00:17:19.085 --> 00:17:19.712
&gt;&gt; It's good stuff.

00:17:19.712 --> 00:17:21.407
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Doug.

00:17:21.407 --> 00:17:22.985
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I gotta tell you,

00:17:22.985 --> 00:17:24.875
if I solved the problems
you get more sales.

00:17:24.875 --> 00:17:27.639
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] So it is a real world problem.

00:17:27.639 --> 00:17:30.007
At the end of the day,
I worry about one thing.

00:17:30.007 --> 00:17:31.841
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Growing my sales at Walmart.

00:17:31.841 --> 00:17:35.180
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But leaving that aside, so that's

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:40.940
an example of how you take design thinking
all the way to the product design itself.

00:17:40.940 --> 00:17:43.990
The other is when you think
about a food service machine,

00:17:43.990 --> 00:17:46.400
in the past with the standard machines.

00:17:46.400 --> 00:17:50.980
And now with the new spire machines
we have which are slowly rolling out.

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:55.590
You can have almost 400, 500 variants.

00:17:55.590 --> 00:18:00.745
But the machine looks
like a beautiful iPad,

00:18:00.745 --> 00:18:05.048
but even more in
an aesthetically pleasing way.

00:18:05.048 --> 00:18:09.849
And so, you bring design into a day to
day experience that was quite boring so

00:18:09.849 --> 00:18:13.089
that's what design thinking
is doing to PepsiCo.

00:18:13.089 --> 00:18:15.667
We got amazing designers we've hired.

00:18:15.667 --> 00:18:20.297
In Milan every year they have
a design week where everything from

00:18:20.297 --> 00:18:23.639
furniture to lighting
to day to day products,

00:18:23.639 --> 00:18:29.680
you see the best of design from the best
designers showcased all around Milan.

00:18:29.680 --> 00:18:36.510
The whole city becomes a design,
sort of a bazaar of designs.

00:18:36.510 --> 00:18:41.390
And it's just a phenomenal experience to
go there and just see what's going on.

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:45.430
In the last few years,
3 years ago I had just visited Milan for

00:18:45.430 --> 00:18:47.905
the first time, from a design perspective.

00:18:47.905 --> 00:18:54.030
Two years ago we started to build a Pepsi
design experience, where we actually had

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:58.600
a big warehouse that we converted design
experiences with PepsiCo products.

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:00.630
So we had an experience
that we called the fizz.

00:19:00.630 --> 00:19:05.790
Looked like Willy Wonka and the Chocolate
Factory, how do you make your own

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:10.690
soda with all kinds of toppings,
just a fabulous experience.

00:19:10.690 --> 00:19:13.800
We had Lipton Pure Leaf Tea.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:17.580
How can you serve tea in an authentic way,
almost like a tea house cafe?

00:19:19.080 --> 00:19:21.868
What can you do with the Gatorade bottle?

00:19:21.868 --> 00:19:25.926
The new bottle with the sensors
that athletes can now drink, and

00:19:25.926 --> 00:19:27.880
it'll, sense your saliva and

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:32.634
tell you exactly, what extra electrolytes
you need to put into your body.

00:19:32.634 --> 00:19:36.640
So we showcase some of the stuff
in a very, uplifting way.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.491
And, what was wonderful
is the big magazines,

00:19:39.491 --> 00:19:43.839
the design magazines wrote that there
were two exhibits that stood out in

00:19:43.839 --> 00:19:46.633
Milan this year from
a consumer perspective.

00:19:46.633 --> 00:19:49.150
One was Nike and the other was PepsiCo.

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.332
And so I thought we'd landed.

00:19:51.332 --> 00:19:52.075
It was a great thing.

00:19:52.075 --> 00:19:53.329
You missed a great one.

00:19:53.329 --> 00:19:53.863
Next year.

00:19:53.863 --> 00:19:54.969
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Next year.

00:19:54.969 --> 00:19:55.805
&gt;&gt; Next year.

00:19:55.805 --> 00:19:57.134
&gt;&gt; That's a lot of progress.

00:19:57.134 --> 00:19:57.737
&gt;&gt; It is.

00:19:57.737 --> 00:19:58.411
&gt;&gt; From one story to another.

00:19:58.411 --> 00:20:01.093
&gt;&gt; And I'd like you to visit
our design studio some time.

00:20:01.093 --> 00:20:02.788
Phenomenal designs.

00:20:02.788 --> 00:20:04.344
We have a long ways to go Doug.

00:20:04.344 --> 00:20:05.133
A long ways to go.

00:20:05.133 --> 00:20:06.390
But they're making progress.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:08.450
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I think it's going to matter to us.

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:12.870
It feels like what's happening in retail
is that the needs that customers have

00:20:13.950 --> 00:20:18.190
are being outsourced to the Internet
whether it's mobile or voice or

00:20:18.190 --> 00:20:22.460
whatever comes next in the future
increasingly, so people are not going to

00:20:22.460 --> 00:20:26.980
have to think about staying in stock
on Pepsi, it's just going to happen.

00:20:26.980 --> 00:20:30.500
And we're going to play a role in that
process through our supply chain.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:31.990
&gt;&gt; But even on something like a Pepsi,

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:38.119
if we keep thinking of it as
the cola that was invented in 1893.

00:20:39.930 --> 00:20:43.500
That's like being
the custodians of a utility.

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:45.310
That is not the business we are in.

00:20:45.310 --> 00:20:48.260
Question is how do you bring excitement
into this category again and again?

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:51.830
Because this is a heritage brand,
so we're doing several things.

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:56.330
Whole range of craft sodas,
lower sugar offerings,

00:20:56.330 --> 00:21:02.620
trying to make Pepsi more of
make my own beverage at home.

00:21:02.620 --> 00:21:05.080
So we're playing with Pepsi in so
many ways.

00:21:05.080 --> 00:21:09.640
1893, the new, cola that we launched,
is a craft soda.

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:11.730
Doing very well where it is.

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:15.190
The make my own Pepsi that's being sold,

00:21:15.190 --> 00:21:17.850
interesting consumer
base it's bringing in.

00:21:17.850 --> 00:21:21.340
And the lower sugar offerings
of Pepsi with great taste,

00:21:21.340 --> 00:21:22.650
will go into to the market.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:25.260
Will they all be as block
buster as the original Pepsi?

00:21:25.260 --> 00:21:25.920
Probably not.

00:21:25.920 --> 00:21:30.260
But they will stem the decline of
a category while we launch all kinds of

00:21:30.260 --> 00:21:31.780
new beverages on top.

00:21:31.780 --> 00:21:33.390
So-
&gt;&gt; The emotional component,

00:21:33.390 --> 00:21:35.313
not just the utilitarian
aspect of your product.

00:21:35.313 --> 00:21:36.605
&gt;&gt; That's exactly right.

00:21:36.605 --> 00:21:40.274
Yeah, because Pepsi is not a drink,
it's an experience,

00:21:40.274 --> 00:21:43.331
it's a brand that as time,
timely I would say,

00:21:43.331 --> 00:21:48.450
because it defines culture,
it defines sports, it defines music.

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:53.730
And, I still remember a famous music
producer in California once said to me,

00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:54.950
when I ran into him he said,

00:21:54.950 --> 00:22:00.240
I don't have any young musical artists
that are worthy of a Pepsi contract.

00:22:00.240 --> 00:22:03.180
That's a big statement,
that are worthy of a Pepsi contract.

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:06.660
Because, to be associated with Pepsi,
that launched so

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:08.540
many young faces was a big thing.

00:22:08.540 --> 00:22:13.050
Whether it was Micheal Jackson, or Britney
Spears, or Madonna, or Christina Aguilera,

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:19.190
they all made their success working with
Pepsi, they were known as Pepsi stars.

00:22:19.190 --> 00:22:22.790
And so, he said I don't have any young
people that are worthy of Pepsi contracts

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:30.080
so the Pepsi brand is absolutely
associated with culture-defining moments.

00:22:30.080 --> 00:22:32.200
And so, we have to figure
out how to make the product

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:37.200
come in tune with the brand even
more as the consumer changes.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:40.870
&gt;&gt; You can probably pick up on some of the
themes, but what happens is as companies

00:22:40.870 --> 00:22:45.070
get older and larger, they start
specializing within the business.

00:22:45.070 --> 00:22:47.570
And it replicates what you've done before.

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:52.159
So in Walmart, we have logisticians,
and merchants, and marketers, and

00:22:52.159 --> 00:22:56.893
lawyers, and everybody specializes and
you end up with these silos that are,

00:22:56.893 --> 00:23:01.580
by design in some instances, created
to just keep doing what you're doing.

00:23:01.580 --> 00:23:05.864
And then, disruption happens, and you have
to figure out how to take an older company

00:23:05.864 --> 00:23:09.316
and a larger company and change it
back to being more entrepreneurial

00:23:09.316 --> 00:23:10.882
move with speed and change it.

00:23:10.882 --> 00:23:14.122
And you can hear those names and
then their story and

00:23:14.122 --> 00:23:18.490
it's the same on our story and
I think a lot of what we're believing at

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:23.482
the moment is that we're going to help
transform the company using technology.

00:23:23.482 --> 00:23:27.684
We can hire talent, we have hired talent,
we can invest, and do a lot of that.

00:23:27.684 --> 00:23:32.553
But over time, what will differentiate us,
and ultimately enable us to win and

00:23:32.553 --> 00:23:35.542
have a role to play in
society is still humanity.

00:23:35.542 --> 00:23:41.381
It's still our culture, it's still our
people, but getting from where we are now

00:23:41.381 --> 00:23:47.232
to there will require cultural change in
addition to advancements in technology.

00:23:47.232 --> 00:23:51.959
And that's an interesting business
challenge to take on when you at our

00:23:51.959 --> 00:23:52.521
scale.

00:23:52.521 --> 00:23:56.969
&gt;&gt; I think the big question for all of us,
Douglas, there's been a lot written about,

00:23:56.969 --> 00:23:58.850
you've got to preserve history and

00:23:58.850 --> 00:24:01.748
the heritage of the company
while you make the change.

00:24:01.748 --> 00:24:03.931
Sometimes I wonder if it's a ballast.

00:24:03.931 --> 00:24:07.802
I'll tell you why, because, yeah,
you should respect the heritage.

00:24:07.802 --> 00:24:09.170
You should respect the culture.

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:14.130
Be cognizant of where you came from but,
I'll speak for

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:19.580
myself, I'd say maybe I was a little
too respectful of the heritage and

00:24:19.580 --> 00:24:22.660
culture because what happens is.

00:24:22.660 --> 00:24:25.880
When you have companies where people
are in the company for long time and

00:24:25.880 --> 00:24:28.890
PepsiCo's got a lot of
long tenured employees.

00:24:28.890 --> 00:24:30.220
They've come into PepsiCo young.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:32.060
They retire from the company, and

00:24:32.060 --> 00:24:35.170
then as they leave the company,
they still remain attached to the company.

00:24:35.170 --> 00:24:38.260
And they are talking about
PepsiCo all the time.

00:24:38.260 --> 00:24:40.010
At some point you've
got to make the break.

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:41.420
You know what I'm talking about.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:42.670
They still think they're
part of the company,

00:24:42.670 --> 00:24:44.520
they're running the company, right?

00:24:44.520 --> 00:24:48.070
And so,
this book club has to be kept happy.

00:24:48.070 --> 00:24:50.800
But I don't think you should
let them run the company.

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:54.520
And in many ways you've got to
make a break with the past.

00:24:54.520 --> 00:25:00.781
Because if you don't, you're spending more
time appeasing the heritage employees.

00:25:00.781 --> 00:25:05.610
And the heritage voices as opposed to
saying guys we don't have the time

00:25:05.610 --> 00:25:07.221
we have to make the change.

00:25:07.221 --> 00:25:07.740
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:25:07.740 --> 00:25:08.470
&gt;&gt; And guess what?

00:25:08.470 --> 00:25:10.120
it's going to be painful, but

00:25:10.120 --> 00:25:15.410
the sooner we make the change let's do it
and the one thing I learned again I'd say

00:25:15.410 --> 00:25:17.650
I wasn't may be a little bit
more patient than I should have.

00:25:18.950 --> 00:25:23.460
When you know you have to make
a change and people don't come along.

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:25.230
You wait for little while, but

00:25:25.230 --> 00:25:29.770
then after that a some point you've
got to say enough is enough.

00:25:29.770 --> 00:25:33.960
Every third week there's going to be
a series of retirement parties you're out,

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:38.320
because if we don't do that, again the
people who have been in the company 20,

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:39.890
30 years pull you down.

00:25:41.040 --> 00:25:45.480
And they create sort of environments
of saying the good old days were great.

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:48.800
And people start to believe that
there's still those good old days that

00:25:48.800 --> 00:25:51.330
could exist, and they really can't.

00:25:51.330 --> 00:25:54.460
And I think,
if I had to do it all over again,

00:25:54.460 --> 00:25:57.950
I might have hastened
the pace of change even more.

00:25:57.950 --> 00:26:00.670
&gt;&gt; Seize that opportunity to
actively shape the culture.

00:26:00.670 --> 00:26:04.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and then say you know what,
guys I'm not going to sit here

00:26:05.270 --> 00:26:09.470
doing workshop after workshop and
trying to convince you to come along.

00:26:09.470 --> 00:26:12.930
I've tried, you're either in it or
you're out.

00:26:12.930 --> 00:26:18.340
And that sort of brute
actions I didn't take.

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:21.310
&gt;&gt; One of the things that gives us
a chance to make it through this

00:26:21.310 --> 00:26:25.020
transformational period is
that our founder loves change.

00:26:25.020 --> 00:26:29.100
And while we can respect the past,
you must invent the future.

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:33.660
And our founder passed away in 1992,
but the one thing I know for

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:37.260
sure from being around him a little
bit and from all of the stories and

00:26:37.260 --> 00:26:40.510
experiences is that if he were here today,
he would be changing things.

00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:42.120
And it gives you that mandate.

00:26:42.120 --> 00:26:46.050
You can stand in front of a group of
Walmart associates and say the only thing

00:26:46.050 --> 00:26:51.290
that's constant at Walmart is and
the group will say back change.

00:26:51.290 --> 00:26:54.360
And that gives you an opportunity to
say okay, let's talk about what we need

00:26:54.360 --> 00:26:58.920
to change, why we need to change and
what this is aimed towards.

00:26:58.920 --> 00:27:00.650
And if we don't, we're done.

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:02.260
It's only a matter of time.

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:03.170
&gt;&gt; Change is scary.

00:27:03.170 --> 00:27:04.430
Change is very, very scary,

00:27:04.430 --> 00:27:08.370
especially when you're living
the comfort of the large company.

00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:14.179
Where you had fabulous salaries,
benefits, pension plan that was terrific.

00:27:14.179 --> 00:27:17.128
And all of a sudden you're
going to be cast out.

00:27:17.128 --> 00:27:19.148
&gt;&gt; Isn't it interesting,
it's perceived safety.

00:27:19.148 --> 00:27:21.670
But it's actually the opposite.

00:27:21.670 --> 00:27:24.230
This safety of a career, of a company,

00:27:24.230 --> 00:27:29.890
the future is actually on the change
side of things not on the status quo.

00:27:29.890 --> 00:27:34.250
&gt;&gt; But if you know that you have
a role in the change you embrace it.

00:27:34.250 --> 00:27:36.460
But if you don’t know if you
have a role in the change or

00:27:36.460 --> 00:27:39.670
because you’re sure you don’t have a role
because you don’t have the skills.

00:27:39.670 --> 00:27:40.600
That’s a problem.

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:42.640
&gt;&gt; So communication today is different.

00:27:42.640 --> 00:27:43.140
&gt;&gt; Totally.

00:27:44.360 --> 00:27:46.020
&gt;&gt; I joined Facebook last week.

00:27:46.020 --> 00:27:48.650
I've been on Instagram for a while.

00:27:48.650 --> 00:27:49.734
What do I need to be on next?

00:27:49.734 --> 00:27:51.382
&gt;&gt; Snapchat.

00:27:51.382 --> 00:27:52.880
&gt;&gt; Snapchat's next.

00:27:52.880 --> 00:27:55.180
There'll be something after that probably.

00:27:55.180 --> 00:27:58.130
But Sam Walton was not on Instagram or

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:02.670
Facebook and I know I'm a little late to
the party, but there's power in it and

00:28:02.670 --> 00:28:06.620
it's all about getting the word out on why
are we doing what we are doing and and

00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:08.060
what is it that we're
trying to accomplish?

00:28:08.060 --> 00:28:11.810
In giving people a voice, you know,
communication is not one directional,

00:28:11.810 --> 00:28:13.100
it's multidirectional.

00:28:13.100 --> 00:28:14.970
&gt;&gt; I'm just on Twitter and
I'm scared to death.

00:28:14.970 --> 00:28:17.550
Because I have no idea
what's going to happen.

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:20.138
Look, for you kids,
you're reckless, all right.

00:28:20.138 --> 00:28:22.460
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; For us, for

00:28:22.460 --> 00:28:28.760
our CEO it's terrifying because if you
make one mistake on tweeting one word out.

00:28:28.760 --> 00:28:33.100
They'll be millions of people who sort
of kill you in the public domain about

00:28:33.100 --> 00:28:35.710
how dare a CEO say what they do.

00:28:35.710 --> 00:28:38.770
I'm envious of my kids because
the stuff they tweet out,

00:28:38.770 --> 00:28:40.400
the stuff they put on Facebook.

00:28:40.400 --> 00:28:41.820
My God, scares me to death, but

00:28:41.820 --> 00:28:44.920
you know what this is the great
thing about being young.

00:28:44.920 --> 00:28:47.660
What they don't know yet
is all of that is going to follow them for

00:28:47.660 --> 00:28:48.614
the rest of their lives.

00:28:48.614 --> 00:28:50.376
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:28:50.376 --> 00:28:52.604
&gt;&gt; That's the tough you know?

00:28:52.604 --> 00:28:54.737
You and
I don't have anything to worry about.

00:28:54.737 --> 00:29:00.130
[LAUGH]
Ted, I wanted to ask you a question.

00:29:00.130 --> 00:29:04.270
How do you manage to be CEO of Walmart,

00:29:04.270 --> 00:29:09.135
living in this small town where every move
that you make is tracked all the time?

00:29:09.135 --> 00:29:12.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] What do you mean tracked?

00:29:12.360 --> 00:29:14.490
You mean everybody knows everybody?

00:29:14.490 --> 00:29:15.145
Like Mayberry?

00:29:15.145 --> 00:29:17.600
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Something like that.

00:29:17.600 --> 00:29:19.610
But we're bigger than Mayberry, yes?

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:22.570
&gt;&gt; Yeah, we live in a cool place
that's actually changing a lot, but

00:29:22.570 --> 00:29:25.110
it's still small and it's a bubble.

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:29.670
It's not like the rest of the world in
some ways and so it's really important for

00:29:29.670 --> 00:29:34.170
us as leaders to be out and
we travel a lot, we're all over the world,

00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:38.060
but go back to this really small
place where you can go to Walmart,

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:41.520
which I do,
everybody's like hey Doug how you doing?

00:29:41.520 --> 00:29:43.711
People follow you around the store to
make sure that everything was in stock.

00:29:43.711 --> 00:29:48.080
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:50.310
we have to be cognizant of the bubble.

00:29:50.310 --> 00:29:53.280
And not let it cause us to be comfortable.

00:29:53.280 --> 00:29:55.120
It's not the most competitive
market in the world.

00:29:56.740 --> 00:29:57.610
&gt;&gt; That's very true.

00:29:57.610 --> 00:30:00.810
You know when I go in market
research it's the same thing.

00:30:00.810 --> 00:30:05.460
They set up the market for me so
I always see the perfect execution.

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:07.530
So now when I go to any city and they say,

00:30:07.530 --> 00:30:11.590
we're going to the following three
streets to do marketer I go, guess what.

00:30:11.590 --> 00:30:13.630
I'm going to decide where we're going.

00:30:13.630 --> 00:30:16.360
And now they're terrified because
I could pick a place that's

00:30:16.360 --> 00:30:18.750
completely off the beaten path and

00:30:18.750 --> 00:30:22.300
I don't want to see a prepared market
I want to see the market as it exists.

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:23.570
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; Normally for the consumer.

00:30:23.570 --> 00:30:25.150
&gt;&gt; That's when we don't
tell stores we're coming.

00:30:25.150 --> 00:30:26.690
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; When yesterday when I popped

00:30:26.690 --> 00:30:28.110
into the store.

00:30:28.110 --> 00:30:31.190
One associate once told me Doug you're
just like the Queen of England.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:33.340
And I said in what way?

00:30:33.340 --> 00:30:35.650
And they said everywhere you
go you smell fresh paint.

00:30:35.650 --> 00:30:39.750
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] That's a good one.

00:30:39.750 --> 00:30:41.140
&gt;&gt; You gotta remember that, right.

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:44.190
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; You gotta remember that and stay real.

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:46.980
I think we've got time for
maybe one more subject and

00:30:46.980 --> 00:30:50.820
the one I'd like to pick is
making a difference in the world.

00:30:50.820 --> 00:30:52.570
So you ended up in this position,

00:30:52.570 --> 00:30:56.070
probably growing up as a kid you knew
I'm going to be the CEO of PepsiCo.

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:58.060
I mean you probably knew that.

00:30:58.060 --> 00:31:01.550
But now you find yourself here,
and you have this responsibility

00:31:02.820 --> 00:31:06.610
in this platform really to try and make
a positive difference in the world, how do

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:12.140
you think about that, and how do you help
lead the board and your management team

00:31:12.140 --> 00:31:14.739
towards making that difference and
what's that mean to you personally?

00:31:15.748 --> 00:31:20.110
&gt;&gt; You know Doug, the more I deal with
this whole idea of making a difference,

00:31:20.110 --> 00:31:25.370
sustainability, I'm convinced that unless
you feel it, unless you feel it deep down

00:31:25.370 --> 00:31:29.240
in your heart and you really understand
it, you'll be doing good service.

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:31.730
That's the whole idea of sustainability.

00:31:31.730 --> 00:31:36.410
In my case, I grew up in Madras
in India where those days and

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:39.770
even today I think there
wasn't much water in the city.

00:31:39.770 --> 00:31:45.340
And we had so little water to
live on every day, that this

00:31:45.340 --> 00:31:51.035
whole notion of water availability and
water use was a crisis for me all my life.

00:31:51.035 --> 00:31:56.446
I didn't know a time when water was
aplenty until I came to the United States,

00:31:56.446 --> 00:31:56.956
okay.

00:31:56.956 --> 00:31:59.209
So water was seared into my head.

00:31:59.209 --> 00:32:03.064
The second is we grew up eating and
drinking a certain way.

00:32:03.064 --> 00:32:06.681
And all of a sudden I realized
that as you look around the world,

00:32:06.681 --> 00:32:10.163
as you look around all of
the health issues around the world,

00:32:10.163 --> 00:32:14.340
food played a very important role in
the people's health and wellness.

00:32:14.340 --> 00:32:18.260
So, one had to worry
about that aspect too.

00:32:18.260 --> 00:32:23.360
And then my daughter went to work for
the environmental defense fund, EDF, and

00:32:23.360 --> 00:32:28.920
I got a whole new appreciation for all of
the environmental issues beyond water.

00:32:28.920 --> 00:32:31.480
Whether it was the carbon footprint,
whether it was fisheries,

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:37.350
whether it was any issue, she would
actually show me all of the statistics,

00:32:37.350 --> 00:32:38.560
what it was doing to the world.

00:32:38.560 --> 00:32:42.230
Overfishing in certain coasts or
plastic in oceans.

00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:45.030
And when you start to really
feel these issues and

00:32:45.030 --> 00:32:47.500
think of what your kids and their kids.

00:32:47.500 --> 00:32:52.240
You ask yourself, what is my role
as a steward of a large company?

00:32:52.240 --> 00:32:56.330
To make a difference in the world through
this incredible platform that I have

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:59.990
called leader of a big company.

00:32:59.990 --> 00:33:04.540
That could either contribute to
the issues or help alleviate the issues.

00:33:05.990 --> 00:33:08.710
I knew that I didn't want to
contribute to the issues.

00:33:08.710 --> 00:33:14.600
I had to start helping the issues and
to the extent that society had changed and

00:33:14.600 --> 00:33:16.830
I was perceived to contributing
to some of the issues,

00:33:16.830 --> 00:33:19.730
I had to make a change
to my business model.

00:33:19.730 --> 00:33:23.624
Therein was born our notion of
performance of purpose which is,

00:33:23.624 --> 00:33:27.158
how can we keep performing
while changing the portfolio,

00:33:27.158 --> 00:33:30.997
while fundamentally changing
our environmental footprint.

00:33:30.997 --> 00:33:32.076
And then third one,

00:33:32.076 --> 00:33:36.266
which is really the one that I am really
most excited about is, what can we do for

00:33:36.266 --> 00:33:40.331
our employees so that they can come to
work at PepsiCo feeling like PepsiCo is

00:33:40.331 --> 00:33:44.250
a place where you cannot just
make a living, but have a life.

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:48.720
So that you can bring your whole self to
work, the company treats you in a fair and

00:33:48.720 --> 00:33:52.950
equitable way, provides you
the support system to be who you are,

00:33:52.950 --> 00:33:54.880
to allow you to be who you are.

00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:56.940
And we embrace you completely.

00:33:56.940 --> 00:34:02.450
And so, performance and purpose is not
a corporate social responsibility program.

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:03.420
It is not.

00:34:03.420 --> 00:34:05.300
It is how we make money.

00:34:05.300 --> 00:34:08.350
Because our belief is that if
you don't change the portfolio,

00:34:08.350 --> 00:34:10.640
we can't keep delivering performance.

00:34:10.640 --> 00:34:13.380
If we don't change our
environmental practices,

00:34:13.380 --> 00:34:16.040
we won't get a license
from society to operate.

00:34:16.040 --> 00:34:18.735
And our cost goes up because we pay for
water.

00:34:18.735 --> 00:34:21.410
We'll end up paying more for
plastic recycling.

00:34:21.410 --> 00:34:22.240
So we have to do that.

00:34:22.240 --> 00:34:23.660
And if we don't get the best and

00:34:23.660 --> 00:34:27.070
brightest in terms of people,
we won't be able to deliver performance.

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:32.080
So performance and purposes how we make
money, not how we spend the money we make.

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:34.980
Which is what corporate social
responsibility is all about.

00:34:34.980 --> 00:34:36.540
So that's really what we are all about.

00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:37.240
&gt;&gt; Very well said.

00:34:37.240 --> 00:34:38.350
We see it the same way.

00:34:38.350 --> 00:34:39.850
I feel the same way.

00:34:39.850 --> 00:34:41.630
We want to open it up for questions.

00:34:41.630 --> 00:34:44.570
Fire a difficult one to Indra
if you could to start us off.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:48.405
&gt;&gt; The first question comes from Twitter,
it's actually to you both and

00:34:48.405 --> 00:34:49.387
it's a classic.

00:34:49.387 --> 00:34:53.729
What organization, and
what leader do you most admire and why?

00:34:56.144 --> 00:35:00.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Need time to think.

00:35:00.980 --> 00:35:04.520
I got to be around Sam Walton a little
bit, Sam was the founder of Walmart.

00:35:04.520 --> 00:35:06.620
He's the first person that comes to mind.

00:35:06.620 --> 00:35:09.267
There are a lot of
positive characteristics

00:35:09.267 --> 00:35:12.353
there that we're still
trying to live up to today.

00:35:12.353 --> 00:35:15.137
In terms of who I admire at the moment,

00:35:15.137 --> 00:35:20.770
rather than one person I think I
would characterize a group of people.

00:35:20.770 --> 00:35:24.490
One of the awesome things about what
we get to do is we get to meet a lot of

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:27.920
leaders and what comes to mind is
that there are people out there that

00:35:27.920 --> 00:35:32.160
are genuinely taking risk to try
to make the world a better place.

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:35.535
And this would be a very
logical time to play it safe.

00:35:35.535 --> 00:35:39.270
It'd be a very logical time to try and
stay off the radar screen.

00:35:39.270 --> 00:35:40.990
But sometimes you gotta step up there and

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:44.190
you gotta put some personal equity on
the line and try to change something.

00:35:44.190 --> 00:35:45.990
And there are people that are doing that.

00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:49.870
Some of the CEOs that live in this part of
the world are doing things like that to

00:35:49.870 --> 00:35:54.030
try and improve the world,
and I admire that.

00:35:54.030 --> 00:35:55.909
&gt;&gt; I'm going to give you two.

00:35:55.909 --> 00:35:59.730
One is something that sounds tested and
tried.

00:35:59.730 --> 00:36:02.080
When I became CEO, I called Steve Jobs and

00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:05.330
I said, I'd like to spend
a little bit of time with you.

00:36:05.330 --> 00:36:12.220
Just to sort of understand what it is to
run a company the way you have changed it.

00:36:12.220 --> 00:36:14.500
And he graciously agreed
to spend some time with me.

00:36:14.500 --> 00:36:17.780
And he gave me two or three hours,
I think, of his time.

00:36:17.780 --> 00:36:20.930
And some of the lessons he
taught me about design.

00:36:20.930 --> 00:36:24.550
How do you take something that you
truly believe in, and stick with it?

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:27.480
As opposed to changing your point of
view because the outside world wants

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:28.690
you to change your point of view.

00:36:30.280 --> 00:36:33.580
Phenomenal lessons, that he was so
gracious to give me the time.

00:36:33.580 --> 00:36:36.110
And that he gave me those
lessons I'll never forget.

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:38.710
I think the world lost
an incredible person in him.

00:36:38.710 --> 00:36:41.280
He did tell me a few things
like don't be too nice.

00:36:41.280 --> 00:36:43.229
When you really don't
get what you want and

00:36:43.229 --> 00:36:45.883
you really believe it's
the right thing for the company,

00:36:45.883 --> 00:36:48.935
it's okay to throw a temperature tantrum,
throw things around.

00:36:48.935 --> 00:36:52.100
People will talk about it and
they'll know it's important for you.

00:36:52.100 --> 00:36:54.308
And I think that in itself
was a valuable lesson.

00:36:54.308 --> 00:36:57.070
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; I honestly believe that.

00:36:57.070 --> 00:37:00.440
In terms of people I admire, I tell you
the people I have really begun to admire

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:04.050
these days are people
who head up these NGOs.

00:37:04.050 --> 00:37:08.700
Because they don't make much money,
in fact, they barely make ends meet,

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:12.850
but yet they feel so
deeply committed to a cause.

00:37:12.850 --> 00:37:16.140
They do the research, they go visit
companies in their headquarters,

00:37:16.140 --> 00:37:18.420
they'll do goofy things,
but you know what?

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:23.450
It's goofy if you look at it strictly from
the point of view of, they're bugging me.

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:27.900
But if you look at it through their eyes,
walk a mile in their shoes, you have to be

00:37:27.900 --> 00:37:32.830
proud that they feel so strongly that they
want to make the world a better place.

00:37:32.830 --> 00:37:37.010
So all of these people who run NGOs,
even though one side of me says god,

00:37:37.010 --> 00:37:39.620
I wish they didn't have
such a big megaphone.

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:44.480
The other side of me is so proud of what
they do and I think we're all going to

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:49.024
change and become better companies
because of their leadership.

00:37:49.024 --> 00:37:49.950
&gt;&gt; Who’d like to go next?

00:37:49.950 --> 00:37:50.880
How about right here?

00:37:52.910 --> 00:37:55.790
Hi, my name's Nadu Lawson,
I'm an MBA too here.

00:37:55.790 --> 00:37:59.830
My question for you was, you mentioned
international growth before, and

00:37:59.830 --> 00:38:05.560
I'd love to hear how PepsiCo and Walmart
are thinking about the African market,

00:38:05.560 --> 00:38:08.250
specifically in the mid and long term.

00:38:08.250 --> 00:38:12.890
What do you think you're going to have to
do differently to win the consumers there?

00:38:14.840 --> 00:38:19.550
&gt;&gt; I tell you PepsiCo in Africa doesn't
have as big a footprint as we would like

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:24.900
to have, but I think it's
an actually wide open market in so

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:27.350
many ways and I'll tell you why.

00:38:27.350 --> 00:38:32.230
The African consumer is, the economic
plight of the African consumer

00:38:32.230 --> 00:38:35.140
is improving enormously, so
that's a very good thing.

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:39.760
Second, the continent is hungry for
the right supply chains,

00:38:39.760 --> 00:38:41.700
the right products to come in.

00:38:41.700 --> 00:38:46.860
So I think the market is
just now sort of taking off.

00:38:46.860 --> 00:38:49.407
I wish we'd been there earlier, but

00:38:49.407 --> 00:38:54.418
now that we are increasing our footprint
there, I think the challenge we

00:38:54.418 --> 00:38:59.363
have is how do we go into Africa with
healthy products right off the bat.

00:38:59.363 --> 00:39:03.255
And not start with the fun-for-you
products, start with more the good-for-you

00:39:03.255 --> 00:39:06.871
products, the more grains, fruit and
vegetable, stuff that contributes to

00:39:06.871 --> 00:39:10.487
the nutritional quality of the African
consumer which means, we have to setup

00:39:10.487 --> 00:39:14.960
the agricultural supply chains, which is
really what we are working on right now.

00:39:14.960 --> 00:39:19.990
So I think especially the sub
Sahara Africa region because in parts of

00:39:19.990 --> 00:39:22.710
Africa like Tanzania or South Africa or

00:39:22.710 --> 00:39:27.210
Nigeria we're in already, but the rest of
Africa I think we're working on setting

00:39:27.210 --> 00:39:31.200
up the agriculture supply chains and
there's a lot of it available in Africa.

00:39:31.200 --> 00:39:33.050
We think the potential is huge.

00:39:34.290 --> 00:39:36.630
&gt;&gt; I'm really excited about
sub-saharan Africa and

00:39:36.630 --> 00:39:40.020
personally was involved in the investment
that we made there a few years ago,

00:39:40.020 --> 00:39:43.450
in what I believe is the best
management team on the continent.

00:39:43.450 --> 00:39:46.560
The best management team in Africa
from a retail point of view.

00:39:46.560 --> 00:39:50.900
We have various store formats where going
to end up with an important business in

00:39:50.900 --> 00:39:55.238
Nigeria someday, in Kenya, in South
Africa, where most of the business is now

00:39:55.238 --> 00:39:58.380
and we're today in about
12 different markets there.

00:39:58.380 --> 00:40:00.273
Personally, I've been to five or

00:40:00.273 --> 00:40:04.667
six different countries in the region in
the last few years and I feel an energy.

00:40:04.667 --> 00:40:09.186
And an optimism and an opportunity that I
believe will be a very good business for

00:40:09.186 --> 00:40:10.430
us over time.

00:40:10.430 --> 00:40:11.520
We want to be local.

00:40:11.520 --> 00:40:14.470
We decided not buy a 100% of
the business because we wanted

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:17.950
others to be able to benefit from
the business that we were growing there.

00:40:17.950 --> 00:40:22.440
We're sourcing locally to the extent
possible, which is not that hard because

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:25.169
most of it's food and we are not
moving it a great distance anyway.

00:40:26.480 --> 00:40:30.200
So I think there's an opportunity to
grow there for a long period of time and

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:33.760
reach a lot of people and fulfill our
purpose, which is to save people money and

00:40:33.760 --> 00:40:35.110
help them live better.

00:40:35.110 --> 00:40:38.300
But we're going to have to generation-skip
in some ways with technology.

00:40:38.300 --> 00:40:40.690
You, I'm sure,
know how advanced mobile is and

00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:44.650
how advanced some things like payment
are and some parts of Sub-Saharan Africa.

00:40:44.650 --> 00:40:47.620
And so, just like the rest of the world,
we've gotta be on the front edge,

00:40:47.620 --> 00:40:51.410
as it relates to technology,
and do it in an African way.

00:40:51.410 --> 00:40:52.515
I'm very excited about it.

00:40:54.791 --> 00:40:56.840
&gt;&gt; All right, my name's Ben,
I'm an MBA too.

00:40:56.840 --> 00:40:58.054
First of all, thanks so much for coming.

00:40:58.054 --> 00:41:01.235
So you talked about having
to be a bit conservative, or

00:41:01.235 --> 00:41:04.435
the pressures to be conservative,
as the CEO of a big company.

00:41:04.435 --> 00:41:05.805
You have to be conservative on Twitter.

00:41:05.805 --> 00:41:08.855
You have to be conservative
with your investors.

00:41:08.855 --> 00:41:12.795
So having to innovate, to succeed, in this
fast-changing world, how do you resist

00:41:12.795 --> 00:41:17.365
those pressures and what can CEOs like
you do to resist those pressures?

00:41:18.840 --> 00:41:21.850
&gt;&gt; I think personal
conservatism versus taking

00:41:21.850 --> 00:41:23.810
risks with the business
are two different issues.

00:41:23.810 --> 00:41:29.230
So, with innovation, you've got to write
the rules for our products because

00:41:29.230 --> 00:41:33.780
very often, if you really want advantage,
you've gotta be the first mover.

00:41:33.780 --> 00:41:36.640
So I think on the product side and
the product experience side,

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:38.359
we can take as many risks as we want.

00:41:39.530 --> 00:41:42.500
When I was talking conservatism,
I was talking about

00:41:42.500 --> 00:41:46.750
personal messages going out or messages
that we put out on behalf of the company.

00:41:46.750 --> 00:41:50.120
We just got to be careful about
putting up personal messages.

00:41:50.120 --> 00:41:51.900
I tell you something on innovation.

00:41:51.900 --> 00:41:55.050
The bolder we can be, the better.

00:41:55.050 --> 00:41:57.910
The more we can break the rules,
the better off we're going to be.

00:41:57.910 --> 00:42:01.710
Because you know the world
is full of ideas today and

00:42:01.710 --> 00:42:03.428
if we don't do it somebody
else is going to do it.

00:42:03.428 --> 00:42:06.570
In fact, I'd say that little
start ups are capturing a lot

00:42:06.570 --> 00:42:10.100
off the growth today even in our space,
even in the food and beverage space.

00:42:10.100 --> 00:42:11.714
So, in many ways in our company,

00:42:11.714 --> 00:42:15.000
we have to act as if we
are a bunch of little start ups.

00:42:15.000 --> 00:42:17.870
That's what we are,
a bunch of little start ups.

00:42:17.870 --> 00:42:23.290
So, in order to do that,
we have got to allow people to be bold,

00:42:23.290 --> 00:42:26.740
write the rules any way they want, and
that's what they're doing in our company.

00:42:26.740 --> 00:42:30.110
When we have $22 billion brands,
and now we've got another three or

00:42:30.110 --> 00:42:33.480
four in the pipeline,
each of them is a little start up.

00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:35.260
And they're not conservative at all.

00:42:35.260 --> 00:42:37.690
Pepsi is not a conservative brand.

00:42:37.690 --> 00:42:40.510
As an individual, I am.

00:42:40.510 --> 00:42:43.310
I'm sure that next year's going to
be less conservative than me.

00:42:43.310 --> 00:42:46.485
But I am a conservative person,
because I'm terrified of all of you.

00:42:46.485 --> 00:42:51.713
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think the advice I would give you,

00:42:51.713 --> 00:42:54.945
whether you're going to start a business
or you're going to join a business,

00:42:54.945 --> 00:42:56.660
would relate to values.

00:42:56.660 --> 00:43:01.540
And pausing for a little bit of time to
know who you are and what you stand for.

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:04.230
In our case, at Walmart,
we have four core values.

00:43:04.230 --> 00:43:07.440
We respect the individual, we strive for
excellence, we serve the customer and

00:43:07.440 --> 00:43:09.120
we act with integrity.

00:43:09.120 --> 00:43:14.460
Those four core values resonate for
me personally, so it's been quite easy

00:43:14.460 --> 00:43:18.110
to be part of Walmart because those are
the things that I personally believe in.

00:43:18.110 --> 00:43:19.630
And the reason that matters,

00:43:19.630 --> 00:43:23.690
in the context of your question is lots
of things are going to come your way.

00:43:23.690 --> 00:43:27.330
There's going to be an emergency tomorrow,
something's going to come up, and

00:43:27.330 --> 00:43:29.730
I'm going to have to deal with it,
or when you're in these jobs,

00:43:29.730 --> 00:43:31.160
you're going to have to deal with it.

00:43:31.160 --> 00:43:34.610
And we need some sort of
a foundation to stand upon.

00:43:34.610 --> 00:43:39.460
And pausing to remember what are our
values, my personal values, and

00:43:39.460 --> 00:43:44.460
how does my response here
relate in that context.

00:43:44.460 --> 00:43:47.150
If you don't have that foundation,
you're going to be

00:43:47.150 --> 00:43:51.795
moving around trying to figure out
one instance at a time what to do.

00:43:51.795 --> 00:43:54.590
Whether it's a question
about risk-taking or

00:43:54.590 --> 00:43:56.820
how you handle a crisis or something else.

00:43:56.820 --> 00:44:01.560
So it sounds a little bit hokey,
I suppose, to pause and

00:44:01.560 --> 00:44:05.760
write down or think about what
you want your end story to be.

00:44:05.760 --> 00:44:08.530
But I think it’s valuable.

00:44:08.530 --> 00:44:12.960
Pretend that you’re at your
retirement celebration someday.

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:15.060
What do you want them to say about you?

00:44:15.060 --> 00:44:17.470
What legacy do you want to have left?

00:44:17.470 --> 00:44:21.007
And predetermine before you start
that that’s going to be your outcome.

00:44:24.815 --> 00:44:25.814
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:44:26.998 --> 00:44:29.250
&gt;&gt; Not sure who's deciding who goes next.

00:44:29.250 --> 00:44:29.854
&gt;&gt; Hi.

00:44:29.854 --> 00:44:33.335
&gt;&gt; Go for it.
&gt;&gt; Yup, thanks for sharing.

00:44:33.335 --> 00:44:34.055
I have a question.

00:44:34.055 --> 00:44:36.424
So Amazon is a huge competitor,
like if you were-

00:44:36.424 --> 00:44:37.074
&gt;&gt; Who is that?

00:44:37.074 --> 00:44:38.282
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Amazon.

00:44:38.282 --> 00:44:40.487
&gt;&gt; Amazon, got it.

00:44:40.487 --> 00:44:44.557
&gt;&gt; I mean, if both of you
could talk to Bezos right now,

00:44:44.557 --> 00:44:46.601
what would you say to him?

00:44:46.601 --> 00:44:47.692
&gt;&gt; Go on vacation.

00:44:47.692 --> 00:44:51.825
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:44:51.825 --> 00:44:53.195
&gt;&gt; All right.

00:44:53.195 --> 00:44:57.775
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Jeff doesn't need any money, but

00:44:57.775 --> 00:44:59.570
I'd be happy to pay for his vacation.

00:44:59.570 --> 00:45:02.805
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; They are doing such a good job, and,

00:45:02.805 --> 00:45:07.935
as a retailer, and somebody who grew up
serving customers, I admire the innovation

00:45:07.935 --> 00:45:12.690
and the solutions they're bringing to
customers, and I'm challenged by it.

00:45:12.690 --> 00:45:18.390
And part of our culture is to embrace what
competition's doing and learn from it and

00:45:18.390 --> 00:45:23.010
try to move quickly to adopt what we
should adopt within our own company.

00:45:23.010 --> 00:45:27.170
In some cases, that's dangerous because it
can look like following and be following,

00:45:27.170 --> 00:45:29.960
and if you are following somebody you
are only ever going to be second best.

00:45:29.960 --> 00:45:32.670
So you have to, at the same time,
be who you are and

00:45:32.670 --> 00:45:35.780
innovate within the context
of who you are.

00:45:35.780 --> 00:45:39.940
But, clearly, what they and other
e-commerce companies, mobile commerce

00:45:39.940 --> 00:45:43.770
companies, are teaching us is that
technology can be used to solve customer

00:45:43.770 --> 00:45:49.275
problems in a different way, and it saves
them time, it makes life simple and easy.

00:45:49.275 --> 00:45:52.345
And we did that for
a period of time in one way.

00:45:52.345 --> 00:45:54.245
And now we've gotta do it in new ways.

00:45:54.245 --> 00:45:56.185
So I welcome the challenge.

00:45:56.185 --> 00:45:57.235
I like a competition.

00:45:57.235 --> 00:46:00.559
The one thing that I know for
sure is that customers are going to win.

00:46:01.990 --> 00:46:05.370
And they will decide who's here and
who's not here and, thankfully for us,

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:09.050
we don't compete against just one company,
we compete against a lot of companies.

00:46:09.050 --> 00:46:12.510
And what matters the most is how much
we're improving and getting better.

00:46:12.510 --> 00:46:13.320
So I like it.

00:46:13.320 --> 00:46:14.960
Gets me up in the morning and

00:46:14.960 --> 00:46:19.240
I feel challenged and
I wouldn't want it any other way.

00:46:20.480 --> 00:46:21.065
&gt;&gt; You said it all.

00:46:21.065 --> 00:46:25.510
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thanks for coming and sharing.

00:46:25.510 --> 00:46:29.090
My name is Erin Jung,
I'm from China and now MB 1.

00:46:29.090 --> 00:46:30.860
So I have a question for both of you,

00:46:30.860 --> 00:46:35.665
as more personal career development,
so one is for Doug.

00:46:35.665 --> 00:46:39.865
Doug, you have spent all your
career life in Walmart, but

00:46:39.865 --> 00:46:43.135
actually for now in our years,
it's not very common.

00:46:43.135 --> 00:46:46.802
So I really want to know what drives
you to stay with one organization for

00:46:46.802 --> 00:46:47.832
your whole life?

00:46:47.832 --> 00:46:49.852
And then another question is for Indra.

00:46:49.852 --> 00:46:53.569
You have tried different kind of role and
different kind of industry.

00:46:53.569 --> 00:47:00.267
I want to know when you find your passion
and how you get this passion for it.

00:47:00.267 --> 00:47:05.400
Thanks.

00:47:05.400 --> 00:47:07.870
&gt;&gt; I think I've learned from
all the industries I've been in

00:47:07.870 --> 00:47:11.000
because it's ranged from
when I was a consultant,

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:15.550
you learn everything from chemicals
to banking to high tech, healthcare.

00:47:15.550 --> 00:47:16.830
I did everything.

00:47:16.830 --> 00:47:22.550
So it was fantastic to learn strategy
through the eyes of a BCG because

00:47:22.550 --> 00:47:26.570
you realize that strategy
is not industry-specific,

00:47:26.570 --> 00:47:29.980
it's frameworks and how you think
about a business, so I learned that.

00:47:29.980 --> 00:47:32.860
And then through my journeys
at Motorola and ABB and

00:47:32.860 --> 00:47:38.230
then coming to PepsiCo,
just going from high tech to utilities and

00:47:38.230 --> 00:47:42.350
power plants, again, I learned so
many different facets of business.

00:47:42.350 --> 00:47:44.440
When in came to PepsiCo,

00:47:44.440 --> 00:47:49.480
even though I hadn't really worked in the
consumer space, I could think differently.

00:47:49.480 --> 00:47:53.490
And that point when I joined PepsiCo,
PepsiCo needed new thinking.

00:47:53.490 --> 00:47:56.752
So I came in and started to shake
up status quo from day one.

00:47:56.752 --> 00:48:01.222
And PepsiCo gave me the leeway to go
ahead and make all the changes and

00:48:01.222 --> 00:48:04.214
suggest all the changes
that need to be made.

00:48:04.214 --> 00:48:06.255
And I think why I've stayed in PepsiCo and

00:48:06.255 --> 00:48:09.461
why I put down roots in PepsiCo From
the day I joined the company and

00:48:09.461 --> 00:48:12.860
this is one part of the culture
that has not changed.

00:48:12.860 --> 00:48:14.630
I feel like PepsiCo's my company.

00:48:15.780 --> 00:48:19.480
It's embraced me, and now I make
sure we embrace other employees.

00:48:19.480 --> 00:48:23.190
I feel it's my company, it's not
some public company called PepsiCo,

00:48:23.190 --> 00:48:24.500
it's my company.

00:48:24.500 --> 00:48:28.410
Every aspect of the company,
I feel I can change it any time I want.

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:30.030
I felt that way when I
was head of strategy.

00:48:30.030 --> 00:48:32.900
I felt that way when I was CFO,
when I was president.

00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:35.530
I feel that even also as a CEO.

00:48:35.530 --> 00:48:38.650
When you have that sense of
ownership about the company,

00:48:38.650 --> 00:48:41.030
why would you leave
something that you own?

00:48:41.030 --> 00:48:45.170
And so, I put down roots and
I don't regret for a moment

00:48:45.170 --> 00:48:49.330
the 22 years that I've been at Pepsi Co
and it's the greatest ride of my life.

00:48:49.330 --> 00:48:51.600
Whether I was CEO or not is irrelevant.

00:48:51.600 --> 00:48:53.260
It's been the greatest ride of my life.

00:48:56.220 --> 00:49:00.380
&gt;&gt; My dad was a dentist and
dentistry's hard.

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:01.570
None of you are going to be dentists,
right?

00:49:01.570 --> 00:49:02.488
That's why you're here.

00:49:02.488 --> 00:49:03.400
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:04.740
&gt;&gt; He was a dentist for

00:49:04.740 --> 00:49:07.480
40 years and it is hard.

00:49:07.480 --> 00:49:08.270
You get up every day.

00:49:08.270 --> 00:49:10.310
You go to see people who
don't really want to see you.

00:49:10.310 --> 00:49:11.592
They're there, because they need to be.

00:49:11.592 --> 00:49:13.020
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:49:13.020 --> 00:49:15.550
&gt;&gt; And so, I was influenced in a big way

00:49:15.550 --> 00:49:16.880
to try and create a career for

00:49:16.880 --> 00:49:19.940
myself that was flexible,
because I didn't want to be trapped.

00:49:19.940 --> 00:49:23.940
Trained for one job and have to do that
job forever, if I didn't like it, so

00:49:23.940 --> 00:49:25.200
I went for a business degree.

00:49:25.200 --> 00:49:28.820
I went for my MBA and
my MBA was primarily driven, so

00:49:28.820 --> 00:49:31.410
that if I got into a situation where
I wasn't happy, I could change jobs.

00:49:31.410 --> 00:49:35.620
I thought it would help me move but
then, I ended up working at Walmart.

00:49:35.620 --> 00:49:40.210
And I really started just because I needed
a summer job to make money for school and

00:49:40.210 --> 00:49:43.320
I loved it and that's why I'm still here.

00:49:43.320 --> 00:49:44.320
I have had a blast.

00:49:44.320 --> 00:49:46.570
I have gotten to do more
than a dozen different jobs.

00:49:46.570 --> 00:49:50.410
I've worked across countries,
across formats.

00:49:50.410 --> 00:49:58.000
We deal with everything from marketing to
logistics to finance to legal to Africa.

00:49:59.360 --> 00:49:59.990
How fun is that?

00:49:59.990 --> 00:50:02.910
So, when I'm not having any fun anymore,
I won't do this anymore, but for

00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:07.000
more than twenty five years now,
it's been a blast.

00:50:07.000 --> 00:50:10.870
And I've come to care about it
an awful lot and I haven't been bored.

00:50:10.870 --> 00:50:12.170
If I was bored,

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:15.862
I'd do something else or if I'm working
around people I didn't like, I'd leave.

00:50:15.862 --> 00:50:18.350
But there are lot of great
people around where I work.

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:23.000
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much for coming.

00:50:23.000 --> 00:50:25.755
My name is Sarah Foo and
I'm a second year MBA student and

00:50:25.755 --> 00:50:27.860
and I'm told I very lucky
got the last question.

00:50:27.860 --> 00:50:32.345
[LAUGH] So, my question to you is, so

00:50:32.345 --> 00:50:36.195
obviously you talk about a lot
of changes and taking risk.

00:50:36.195 --> 00:50:40.650
But a lot of that is only going to be
measured or seen or evaluated over years.

00:50:40.650 --> 00:50:44.430
But also today, if you look around
the world like investors or

00:50:44.430 --> 00:50:46.800
just the market in general
is very short sided.

00:50:46.800 --> 00:50:48.250
And then, there is that so
much noise in the market.

00:50:48.250 --> 00:50:49.990
So, how do you balance
this short term demand for

00:50:49.990 --> 00:50:52.640
you to deliver versus
the long term vision.

00:50:52.640 --> 00:50:59.630
To take risk and make long term changes
that are actually good for the business?

00:50:59.630 --> 00:51:01.090
That’s really well said.

00:51:01.090 --> 00:51:02.175
&gt;&gt; I know.
&gt;&gt; I think you get it.

00:51:02.175 --> 00:51:05.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Now what do we do about it?

00:51:05.830 --> 00:51:06.910
&gt;&gt; What do we do about it?

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:09.200
You know I’ll tell you something.

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:13.390
I think you got to look at the investments
you make in the company, as a portfolio.

00:51:13.390 --> 00:51:16.370
There’s a bunch of stuff that
delivers in the short term and

00:51:16.370 --> 00:51:20.735
that gives you the breathing room into
further to invest in the long term.

00:51:20.735 --> 00:51:22.980
Because I don't think, especially now,

00:51:22.980 --> 00:51:26.177
industries that are more
established unlike a startup.

00:51:26.177 --> 00:51:30.720
I don't think you can say, look, wait for
three or five years and five years,

00:51:30.720 --> 00:51:32.156
you'll start seeing huge results.

00:51:32.156 --> 00:51:35.480
Nobody is going to believe you,
because we are expected to deliver,

00:51:35.480 --> 00:51:39.840
because we established legacy companies.

00:51:39.840 --> 00:51:41.761
So the way it is,
I look at things in Pepsi,

00:51:41.761 --> 00:51:44.210
because I know what we need to invest in.

00:51:44.210 --> 00:51:47.600
Constantly balancing projects
that will deliver today,

00:51:47.600 --> 00:51:49.690
countries that will deliver today.

00:51:49.690 --> 00:51:54.110
Which can then provide the ammunition for
us to invest in long term initiatives.

00:51:54.110 --> 00:51:56.730
You can't have too many
long term initiatives or

00:51:56.730 --> 00:51:58.020
too many short term initiatives.

00:51:58.020 --> 00:52:01.620
Because you don't want to spoil
the investor with too many short

00:52:01.620 --> 00:52:02.870
term programs.

00:52:02.870 --> 00:52:06.510
That then don't give you the position
to invest of the long term.

00:52:06.510 --> 00:52:11.060
So, this judicious balance is
what we worry about all the time.

00:52:11.060 --> 00:52:15.000
And I can tell you, the only way you can
do that is if you have the support of your

00:52:15.000 --> 00:52:15.870
board of directors.

00:52:15.870 --> 00:52:20.540
For public companies, the board has to
completely buy into your program and

00:52:20.540 --> 00:52:21.140
back you.

00:52:21.140 --> 00:52:25.060
In case investors say,
we don't like the shape of your delivery.

00:52:26.720 --> 00:52:29.180
&gt;&gt; If we ran these businesses for
one quarter of one year,

00:52:29.180 --> 00:52:33.910
we would harm them greatly and
we care too much about them to do that.

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:36.720
But as we're articulating
that long term vision,

00:52:36.720 --> 00:52:41.950
which in some cases may be
beyond one investment cycle.

00:52:41.950 --> 00:52:44.860
We have to communicate clearly
what our strategy is and

00:52:44.860 --> 00:52:47.650
be held accountable to
milestones along the way.

00:52:47.650 --> 00:52:50.440
So it's not enough to say,
I am managing this business for

00:52:50.440 --> 00:52:53.520
the long term get back to me in
five years, we'll see how it goes.

00:52:53.520 --> 00:52:54.825
&gt;&gt; We'd like to try that.

00:52:54.825 --> 00:52:56.340
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:52:56.340 --> 00:53:00.710
I think as long as you are deliberate,
thoughtful, you build relationships.

00:53:00.710 --> 00:53:02.840
And communicate,
whether it's the investment committee or

00:53:02.840 --> 00:53:04.930
your board, you can do it.

00:53:04.930 --> 00:53:05.580
In our case,

00:53:05.580 --> 00:53:10.700
we have a family that owns almost
half the business, so it helps a lot.

00:53:10.700 --> 00:53:15.961
If they believe in what you're doing,
it helps us have a long-term perspective,

00:53:15.961 --> 00:53:18.753
not just a perspective that's
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK].

00:53:18.753 --> 00:53:21.424
&gt;&gt; If you ever have the chance,
I recommend it.

00:53:21.424 --> 00:53:24.358
&gt;&gt; Please join me in thanking Doug and
Indra.

00:53:24.358 --> 00:53:26.874
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:53:26.874 --> 00:53:27.534
&gt;&gt; Thank you very much.

00:53:27.534 --> 00:53:28.886
&gt;&gt; Great job, thank you.

00:53:28.886 --> 00:53:29.397
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:53:29.397 --> 00:53:33.112
[APPLAUSE]

00:53:33.112 --> 00:53:38.570
[MUSIC]

